index dac0ce2b..1d08aac2 100644
--- a/docs/configuration-sensor-groups.md
+++ b/docs/configuration-sensor-groups.md
@@ -54,9 +54,14 @@ Please note:
 ## When a sensor goes silent
 Irrigation Plus checks every five minutes whether the sensors of a sensor group still report. What counts is a sensor's Home Assistant device: as long as any sensor of that device reports, a value that merely stays the same, such as a rain gauge on a dry day, counts as alive. Only sensors of the device's own integration count: a helper attached to the device, such as a utility meter, does not keep the device alive, but a helper you map yourself is covered by the device's sensors. A sensor without a device counts for itself. A sensor whose state is `unavailable` or `unknown` counts as silent whatever its device does. Values from an `input_number` helper never count as silent.
 
-Once a sensor has not reported for three hours, Irrigation Plus shows a repair notice for its sensor group and fires the `irrigation_plus_weather_stale` event (see [Events](usage-events.md)). When the sensor reports again, the notice clears itself and a second event marks the end. Meanwhile the calculation keeps using the sensor's last value.
+Once a sensor has not reported for three hours, Irrigation Plus shows a repair notice for its sensor group and fires the `irrigation_plus_weather_stale` event (see [Events](usage-events.md)). When the sensor reports again, the notice clears itself and a second event marks the end. What the calculation does meanwhile depends on the sensor group's setting **When a sensor goes silent**:
 
-The three hours are fixed. An integration that updates less often, such as a cloud service polled every six hours, therefore raises the notice between its updates even though nothing has failed.
+- **Pause** (the default for a sensor group created from now on): from three hours after the sensor's last report until it reports again, its values are no longer used, and the daily calculation books no evapotranspiration for that time. Rain from a cumulative rain gauge still counts in full, because what the gauge reads after its return was measured, only delivered late. Zones that depend on the silent sensor therefore water less, or not at all, until it reports again. The explanation of the calculation names the silent time and the share that was booked.
+- **Keep the last value** (the setting of every sensor group that existed before): the calculation keeps using the sensor's last value, as it always did.
+
+Pausing applies only without the hourly calculation; with it, the last value is still used. With the live estimate, a run during an outage can be sized on more evapotranspiration than the calculation books afterwards. The daily calculation averages a pausing sensor over the hours in which it reported, so which part of the day was missing can shift the result.
+
+The three hours are fixed. An integration that updates less often, such as a cloud service polled every six hours, therefore raises the notice between its updates even though nothing has failed. With **Pause**, set such a sensor group to **Keep the last value**; otherwise part of the time between its updates is never booked.
 
 A sensor without a device that reports only when its value changes, such as a template sensor or a utility meter set up in YAML that counts rain, looks silent too once the value has stayed the same for three hours, on a dry day for instance. A utility meter set up in the UI belongs to its source's device, if the source has one, and is covered by it. If a value is meant to be fixed, use the "Static value" source instead.
 
